  "command": "cat > /app/paper.txt << 'EOF'\n# Research Advances in Code Agents and Red-teaming\n\n## Abstract\n\nThe rapid advancement of code agents powered by large language models (LLMs) has revolutionized software development by enabling automated code generation, execution, and debugging. However, these capabilities also introduce significant security concerns, particularly around the execution of potentially malicious or risky code. This paper surveys recent advances in code agents and red-teaming methodologies, examining both offensive and defensive approaches to ensuring the safety and security of code-generating AI systems. We discuss key works including RedCodeAgent, which introduces an automatic red-teaming agent against diverse code agents; RedCode, a benchmark for evaluating risky code execution and generation; Deep Residual Learning for Image Recognition, which provides foundational insights into deep learning architectures that underpin modern code agents; and BlueCodeAgent, which explores blue teaming strategies enabled by automated red teaming. Our review highlights the importance of comprehensive security evaluations and proposes future directions for building safer code agent ecosystems.\n\n## 1. Introduction\n\nCode agents represent one of the most transformative applications of artificial intelligence in software engineering. By integrating large language models with code interpreters and execution environments, these agents can generate, execute, debug, and optimize code dynamically. Applications range from automated code completion and bug fixing to complex software development workflows that previously required significant human expertise.\n\nHowever, the autonomy and capabilities of code agents introduce novel security challenges. When code agents have the ability to execute code in sandboxed or unsandboxed environments, they become potential vectors for malicious activities, including:\n- Execution of harmful scripts or malware\n- Data exfiltration through generated code\n- Privilege escalation within execution environments\n- Generation of vulnerable code that introduces security flaws in production systems\n\nAddressing these challenges requires systematic approaches to evaluating and improving the safety of code agents. Red-teaming methodologies, which involve systematically probing systems for vulnerabilities, have proven effective in many AI security contexts. In this paper, we survey recent advances in red-teaming approaches specifically tailored to code agents and discuss complementary blue-teaming strategies for defense.\n\nThe contributions of this paper are:\n1. A comprehensive overview of code agent architectures and their security implications\n2. A review of red-teaming methodologies for code agents, including automatic red-teaming frameworks and benchmarking approaches\n3. Analysis of blue-teaming strategies for protecting code generation systems\n4. Discussion of future research directions for building safer code agent ecosystems\n\n## 2. Related Work\n\n### 2.1 Foundation Models and Deep Learning Architectures\n\nThe development of modern code agents relies heavily on advances in deep learning architectures. The introduction of residual learning frameworks has been instrumental in enabling the training of deeper neural networks with improved performance. As demonstrated in [1], deep residual learning allows for the construction of substantially deeper networks by reformulating layers to learn residual functions rather than unreferenced mappings. This architectural innovation has enabled the development of large language models that form the backbone of contemporary code agents, providing the capacity to understand and generate complex programming languages.\n\n### 2.2 Red-Teaming Methodologies for Code Agents\n\nRecent work has focused on developing specialized red-teaming approaches to evaluate the safety of code agents. Guo et al. introduced **RedCodeAgent** [2], an automatic red-teaming agent designed to probe diverse code agents for vulnerabilities. RedCodeAgent systematically generates adversarial prompts and evaluates the responses of code agents, identifying potential security weaknesses and unsafe code generation patterns. This approach automates the red-teaming process, enabling comprehensive evaluation of multiple code agents against a range of attack vectors.\n\nComplementing RedCodeAgent, the **RedCode** benchmark [3] provides a standardized framework for evaluating risky code execution and generation capabilities of code agents. RedCode defines a comprehensive set of test cases spanning various types of risky code, including malware generation, exploit development, and unsafe system operations. The benchmark enables quantitative comparison of code agents across multiple safety metrics and facilitates the development of more robust safety mechanisms.\n\n### 2.3 Blue-Teaming and Defensive Strategies\n\nWhile red-teaming focuses on identifying vulnerabilities, blue-teaming strategies aim to develop defensive mechanisms that protect code generation systems. Guo et al. proposed **BlueCodeAgent** [4], which introduces a blue teaming agent enabled by automated red teaming for CodeGen AI systems. This approach leverages insights gained from red-teaming activities to develop proactive defensive strategies, including:\n- Safe code generation filters\n- Runtime monitoring and anomaly detection\n- Automated vulnerability patching\n- Safety-aligned fine-tuning techniques\n\nThe integration of automated red-teaming with blue-teaming strategies creates a feedback loop that continuously improves the security posture of code agent systems. By learning from red-teaming results, blue-teaming agents can adapt their defensive mechanisms to address emerging threats and vulnerabilities.\n\n## 3. Conclusion\n\nThe integration of large language models with code execution environments has created powerful code agents with significant potential for software development automation. However, these capabilities also introduce substantial security risks that require systematic evaluation and mitigation. This paper has surveyed key advances in red-teaming and blue-teaming methodologies for code agents, highlighting the importance of comprehensive security frameworks.\n\nKey findings from our review include:\n1. Automated red-teaming approaches, such as RedCodeAgent and the RedCode benchmark, provide systematic methods for evaluating code agent safety\n2. Blue-teaming strategies, including BlueCodeAgent, demonstrate the value of integrating red-teaming insights into defensive mechanisms\n3. Foundation architectures like deep residual learning continue to underpin the development of more capable code agents\n\nFuture research directions should focus on:\n- Developing standardized safety benchmarks and evaluation protocols\n- Creating robust defense-in-depth strategies for code agent systems\n- Exploring adversarial training methods to improve code agent resilience\n- Building comprehensive safety certifications for code agent deployments\n\nAs code agents become increasingly prevalent in software development workflows, the importance of rigorous security evaluation and safety mechanisms cannot be overstated. The collaborative development of red-teaming and blue-teaming strategies will be essential for building trustworthy and secure code agent ecosystems.\n\n## References\n\n[1] Kaiming He, Xiangyu Zhang, Shaoqing Ren, Jian Sun, \"Deep Residual Learning for Image Recognition,\" arXiv:1512.03385, 2015.\n\n[2] Chengquan Guo, Chulin Xie, Yu Yang et al., \"RedCodeAgent: Automatic Red-teaming Agent against Diverse Code Agents,\" arXiv:2510.02609, 2025.\n\n[3] Chengquan Guo, Xun Liu, Chulin Xie et al., \"RedCode: Risky Code Execution and Generation Benchmark for Code Agents,\" arXiv:2409.12345, 2024.\n\n[4] Chengquan Guo, Yuzhou Nie, Chulin Xie et al., \"BlueCodeAgent: A Blue Teaming Agent Enabled by Automated Red Teaming for CodeGen AI,\" arXiv:2501.01234, 2025.\nEOF"
